[Unit]
Description=Sofia PT archive compaction (gzip closed day files)

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# No shell involved: systemd execs ExecStart directly, and a bare
# `find ... -exec gzip {} +` needs no shell — no wrapper script to keep in
# sync with this unit, no quoting-through-a-shell surprises.
#
# -mtime +2 is the safety margin, not +0 or +1. GNU find truncates the age to
# whole days before comparing, so this actually matches files at least THREE
# days old, one day more conservative than it reads: verified on the VPS at
# 2026-08-31 14:22 UTC, where it selected only 2026-08-27.jsonl and left
# 2026-08-28.jsonl (2.7 days old) alone. collect.py
# rotates at local Sofia midnight but this box's clock and the timer's own
# schedule are both UTC, and scheduled_fetch.sh / fetch_data.sh can still be
# mid-pull on the laptop side. Two full days of slack guarantees this never
# gzips a file collect.py could still be appending to — see CLAUDE.md's
# manifest-describes-uncompressed-bytes invariant, which depends on a day
# file being genuinely closed before anything touches it.
#
# gzip rewrites the file in place (drops the original, keeps the same
# basename plus .gz) — nothing downstream needs to know which form a given
# day is in, see config.resolve_day_file() on the fetch side.
#
# -6 is spelled out rather than relying on gzip's own default (which is
# also 6) so this unit still does the right thing if that default is ever
# overridden system-wide via the GZIP env var — this is the ratio the disk
# math in CLAUDE.md section 9 (~102 MB/day -> ~12 MB/day) was measured with.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/find /opt/sofia-pt/data -type f ( -name '*.jsonl' -o -name '*.polls.jsonl' ) -mtime +2 -exec /usr/bin/gzip -6 {} +
User=root
